concepts.body:
Tailwind v4 lets you add utilities and variants that behave exactly like the built-in ones: they work with every variant, sort correctly and land in the utilities layer.

**Static utilities** with `@utility`:

```css
@utility content-auto { content-visibility: auto; }
```

Now `content-auto`, `md:content-auto` and `hover:content-auto` all work.

**Functional utilities** end in `-*` and read the value with `--value()`:

```css
@utility tab-* { tab-size: --value(integer); }
```

makes `tab-2`, `tab-4` and so on. `--value(--tab-size-*)` reads from a theme namespace instead, and `--value([integer])` accepts arbitrary values like `tab-[7]`. Several `--value()` forms can be combined as fallbacks in one declaration.

**Custom variants** with `@custom-variant`:

```css
@custom-variant theme-midnight (&:where([data-theme=midnight] *));
```

lets you write `theme-midnight:bg-black`. The block form accepts `@slot` for complex cases, such as wrapping in a media query.

**Using variants in your own CSS**: inside a regular rule, `@variant dark { ... }` applies Tailwind's dark variant to those declarations.

**Plugins**: `@plugin "@tailwindcss/typography";` loads a JavaScript plugin. The official typography plugin provides `prose` for rich text, and the forms plugin resets form controls. A legacy v3 config can still be loaded with `@config "./tailwind.config.js";`.

concepts.code:
/* app.css */
@import "tailwindcss";
@plugin "@tailwindcss/typography";

@utility scrollbar-hidden {
  scrollbar-width: none;
  &::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }
}

@theme { --tab-size-github: 8; }
@utility tab-* {
  tab-size: --value(--tab-size-*, integer, [integer]);  /* theme, number, or [n] */
}

@custom-variant theme-midnight (&:where([data-theme=midnight], [data-theme=midnight] *));

.legacy-widget {
  background: white;
  @variant dark { background: black; }   /* reuse Tailwind's dark variant */
}

/* Markup: <pre class="tab-4 md:tab-github scrollbar-hidden">...</pre>
           <article class="prose theme-midnight:prose-invert">...</article> */

// Button.jsx  (npm install clsx tailwind-merge)
import clsx from "clsx";
import { twMerge } from "tailwind-merge";

const variants = {
  primary: "bg-indigo-600 text-white hover:bg-indigo-700",
  ghost: "bg-transparent text-gray-700 hover:bg-gray-100",
};
const sizes = { sm: "px-2.5 py-1 text-sm", md: "px-4 py-2" };

export function Button({ variant = "primary", size = "md", className, ...props }) {
  return (
    <button
      className={twMerge(clsx("rounded-md font-medium", variants[variant], sizes[size]), className)}
      {...props}
    />
  );
}
// <Button size="sm" className="px-6">Save</Button>
// -> "rounded-md font-medium bg-indigo-600 ... py-1 text-sm px-6" (px-2.5 removed)

/* app.css */
@import "tailwindcss" source("../src");      /* scan from src/ only */
@source "../node_modules/@acme/ui/dist";     /* plus a component library */
@source not "../src/legacy";                 /* but not this folder */
@source inline("bg-{red,amber,green}-{100,500}");  /* safelist CMS colours */

/* In components (JSX), never build names dynamically:
   BAD : <span className={`bg-${status}-500`} />        never generated
   GOOD: const color = { ok: "bg-green-500", warn: "bg-amber-500", error: "bg-red-500" };
         <span className={color[status]} />                          */

/* app.css */
@import "tailwindcss";

@layer components {
  .btn { padding: 0.5rem 1rem; border-radius: 0.375rem; }  /* utilities still win */
}

/* UNLAYERED: beats every Tailwind utility, often by accident */
.card p { margin: 0; }          /* now "mt-4" on those <p> elements does nothing */

/* Safer: put overrides in a layer */
@layer base {
  h1 { font-size: var(--text-3xl); font-weight: 700; }
}

/* Markup:
   <button class="btn px-8">       px-8 wins over .btn's padding (later layer)
   <p class="text-gray-500 text-red-600!">  important modifier forces red */
